---
import BaseLayout from '../../components/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { toolsByCategory } from '../../lib/tools';
import { collectionPageGraph } from '../../lib/schema';
import { siteConfig } from '../../config/site';

const tools = toolsByCategory('sewing');
const title = 'Sewing Calculators: Fabric & Seams';
const description =
  'Free sewing calculators for fabric yardage (garments, curtains, upholstery) and seam allowance or pattern grading helpers. Plan cuts before you buy.';
const jsonLd = collectionPageGraph({
  name: title,
  description,
  path: '/sewing/',
  crumbs: [
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Sewing', path: '/sewing/' },
  ],
});
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/sewing/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="container">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Sewing' }]} />
    <h1>Sewing calculators</h1>
    <p class="takeaway">
      <strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Estimate fabric from project dimensions and fabric width—then refine cut sizes with
      seam allowance. Example: a 48×84″ curtain at 2× fullness on 54″ fabric with 10% waste is about 5.9 yards.
    </p>
    <p class="meta-row"><span>Last reviewed {siteConfig.lastReviewed}</span></p>
    <div class="tool-grid">
      {tools.map((t) => (
        <a class="tool-link" href={t.path}><h2>{t.title}</h2><p>{t.description}</p></a>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What these tools solve</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/tools/fabric-yardage-calculator/">fabric yardage calculator</a> turns finished width and length,
        bolt width, optional pattern repeat, and waste into a shopping length in yards or meters. Use it for garments,
        curtains, upholstery covers, and tablecloths when you need a planning figure before you visit the store. The
        <a href="/tools/seam-allowance-calculator/">seam allowance calculator</a> converts finished measurements to cut
        sizes, grades a measurement between sizes, or adds wearing ease. Neither tool replaces a full commercial
        pattern’s yardage chart; both keep arithmetic local in your browser.
      </p>

      <h2>How to measure before you buy</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Measure the finished width and length you want (window opening for curtains, table top for cloths, body or pattern piece for garments).</li>
        <li>Note the usable fabric width on the bolt (often about 44/45″ or 54/60″ for apparel and home décor—confirm in the store).</li>
        <li>Add hems, headers, and seam allowances before cutting; the tools’ waste and seam fields exist for that buffer.</li>
        <li>If the print has a vertical repeat, enter it so panel lengths can round up to a matching motif.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Worked example: curtain panels</h2>
      <p>
        Window 48″ wide × 84″ drop, 2× fullness, 54″-wide fabric, 10% waste. Total width needed = 48 × 2 = 96″ →
        ceil(96 ÷ 54) = 2 panels. Each panel length ≈ 84 + 10″ hem/header = 94″. Two panels = 188″; ×1.10 waste ≈
        206.8″ ÷ 36 ≈ <strong>5.74–5.9 yards</strong> depending on rounding—matches the takeaway above when you run the
        same inputs in the fabric yardage calculator.
      </p>

      <h2>Worked example: finished to cut size</h2>
      <p>
        A pocket that should finish 5″ wide with ⅝″ seam allowance on both sides needs a cut width of
        5 + 2 × 0.625 = <strong>6.25″</strong>. Use the seam allowance calculator when you are drafting or altering
        rather than following a printed cutting layout.
      </p>

      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Shopping from finished size only and forgetting hem, header, or seam allowance.</li>
        <li>Ignoring directional prints or naps that force extra length for matching.</li>
        <li>Treating garment “coverage factor” estimates as a substitute for a graded pattern chart—always prefer the pattern when you have one.</li>
        <li>Assuming every bolt is 45″ usable; selvages and printing can shrink the usable width.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Guides and related hubs</h2>
      <p>
        When you want yardage explained in words, read
        <a href="/guides/how-many-yards-of-fabric/">how many yards of fabric you need</a>. Quilters often need
        <a href="/guides/how-much-quilt-binding-do-i-need/">binding</a>,
        <a href="/guides/quilt-backing-yardage-guide/">backing</a>, and the
        <a href="/quilting/">quilting hub</a> next. Knitters and crocheters shopping yarn instead of woven fabric should
        start at the <a href="/knitting-crochet/">knitting &amp; crochet hub</a>. Charts for mattress and quilt top
        sizes live under <a href="/reference/quilt-sizes/">quilt &amp; mattress sizes</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Planning assumptions for fabric tools (fullness defaults, hem add-ons, waste) are documented as heuristics on
        <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>. Prefer a published pattern’s tested yardage when it conflicts with a
        calculator estimate.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
